.TH CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_DECLARE_SCOPED 3 "2026-07-12" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_event_loop_declare_scoped \- declare a ccol_event_loop with automatic destruction on scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_event_loop_declare_scoped(name);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares a ccol_event_loop variable initialised to
CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_INVALID and tagged with a GCC/Clang cleanup attribute
(via _ccol_destructor) that calls ___ccol_event_loop_destroy()
automatically when the variable goes out of scope.  The variable must
still be assigned via ccol_event_loop_construct() or a
ccol_event_loop_create*() call before use.

Prefer ccol_event_loop_construct_scoped() when declaration and
initialisation can happen together, which is the common case.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name  Variable name to declare.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Safe to leave the variable CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_INVALID through the whole
scope (the cleanup function is a no-op on that value).
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
{
    ccol_event_loop_declare_scoped(loop);
    char *err = NULL;
    loop = ccol_event_loop_create(32, 1, 1, &err);
    if (!loop) { fprintf(stderr, "%s\en", err); return; }
    /* ... */
}  /* loop is automatically destroyed here */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_event_loop_declare (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_construct_scoped (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_create (3)
